#
# SUPERSEDED -- there is nothing to copy here any more.
#
# The ess-script registry connection is DECLARED, in local/rig.tcl:
#
#     setting registry url       https://dserv.net
#     setting registry workgroup brown-sheinberg
#
# or, without editing anything by hand:
#
#     essctrl -c 'settings::put registry url https://dserv.net -persist'
#     essctrl -c 'settings::put registry workgroup brown-sheinberg -persist'
#
# config/rig_settings.tcl exports the pair to ESS_REGISTRY_URL and
# ESS_WORKGROUP before any subprocess starts, so ess, scripts, sync,
# trialsync and configs read what they always did -- and a put also reaches
# those interps while they run. Empty means NOT CONFIGURED: sync and push
# stay local and trial ingest reports no_workgroup, which is the old
# behavior of having no file here at all.
#
# The same values used to be written in local/mesh.tcl as well
# (`mesh_configure <url> <workgroup>`). Mesh now falls back to the declared
# pair when local/mesh.tcl does not configure one, so that file can go too
# unless this box deliberately meshes somewhere else.
#
# A rig that still has a local/pre-registry.tcl is not broken: on the first
# boot after the upgrade those exports are ADOPTED into local/rig.tcl, and
# from then on the declaration wins and this file does nothing. dserv says so
# at boot ("local/pre-registry.tcl is superseded ..."). Delete it once you
# have seen that line.
#
# NOT covered by the setting: dserv-agent has its own --registry and
# --workgroup flags and is a separate process that must keep working while
# dserv is down. Changing the setting does not move a running agent; its unit
# file has to match, and it has to be restarted.
#
